WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.795
A red cell must
deform to move through microcirculation.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.230
The normal cell and the sickle cell shown here

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.366
are adapted from scholarly illustrations.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.284
The motion uses schematic time and scale.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.840
In sickle cell disease,
abnormal hemoglobin polymerization stiffens the

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.095
cell.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.640
Hypoxia, dehydration and acidosis
favor sickling in the packet.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.460
Stiff cells obstruct small
vessels, causing pain and tissue

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:23.820
injury.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:25.902
Keep the crises distinct.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.220
Vasoocclusive crisis obstructs microcirculation.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.130
Aplastic crisis transiently stops production.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.033
Sequestration crisis pools blood in the spleen.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.784
Hyperhemolytic crisis increases red cell destruction.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.820
The treatment slide links
care to crisis prevention, pain

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.573
and infection management, fluids and hydroxyurea.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.129
Supplemental oxygen is qualified by current hypoxia.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.524
Chapter thirty, slides twenty
three, twenty four, twenty six,

00:00:52.524 --> 00:00:55.200
twenty seven and twenty nine, supply these claims.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.740
The contradictory amino acid
substitution wording is excluded; definitive

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.774
diagnostic testing remains missing.
